{"identifier":"bacteria_friend_and_foe","title":"Bacteria: Friend and Foe","description":"Produced by Encyclopedia Britannica Films, Inc., in collaboration with Stewart A. Koser, Ph.D, Department of Bacteriology and Parasitology, the University of Chicago, Warren Everote, Producer. 0595 PA8087 Bacteria: Friend and Foe","videoUrl":"https://archive.org/download/bacteria_friend_and_foe/bacteria_friend_and_foe_512kb.mp4","thumbnailUrl":"https://archive.org/services/img/bacteria_friend_and_foe","sourcePageUrl":"https://archive.org/details/bacteria_friend_and_foe","durationMs":610000,"level":"C2","topic":"Motion Pictures: In Education;Science;Health and Hygiene","sourceCredit":"Source: Internet Archive — Bacteria: Friend and Foe","hasCaptions":true,"segments":[{"startMs":17000,"endMs":21001,"text":"Ted Jones is visiting one of the laboratories at the City Health Department."},{"startMs":22000,"endMs":26001,"text":"His brother Frank has recently taken a position as a bacteriologist with the department."},{"startMs":28001,"endMs":30001,"text":"Ted seems to be having a little trouble."},{"startMs":31000,"endMs":32001,"text":"Can't you get her focused on the field, Ted?"},{"startMs":36001,"endMs":42000,"text":"Try using the fine adjustment knob."},{"startMs":44000,"endMs":45000,"text":"And do it more slowly."},{"startMs":46000,"endMs":48001,"text":"You're likely to miss the cells you're looking for even though they may be there."},{"startMs":49000,"endMs":50000,"text":"Okay, I'm trying."},{"startMs":54000,"endMs":56000,"text":"Say, here they are."},{"startMs":62001,"endMs":63000,"text":"Hey, look."},{"startMs":66000,"endMs":67000,"text":"Good."},{"startMs":68000,"endMs":69000,"text":"You know what kind they are?"},{"startMs":71000,"endMs":73001,"text":"All I know is that they came out of that test tube that you gave me."},{"startMs":74000,"endMs":77000,"text":"Then they're one of the kinds of bacteria that cause food to spoil."},{"startMs":78001,"endMs":81001,"text":"Ted has just seen one of the many kinds of bacteria."},{"startMs":82000,"endMs":85000,"text":"Bacteria are found almost everywhere in our environment."},{"startMs":86000,"endMs":87001,"text":"Some float around in the air."},{"startMs":88001,"endMs":90001,"text":"Some are in water or other liquids."},{"startMs":93000,"endMs":94001,"text":"Still others thrive in the soil."},{"startMs":95001,"endMs":100000,"text":"Bacteria are plants, but they are not green like the plants we usually see."},{"startMs":100001,"endMs":102000,"text":"Some kinds are indispensable."},{"startMs":107000,"endMs":111000,"text":"For example, countless bacteria are at work in this clover field."},{"startMs":111001,"endMs":114001,"text":"They are growing in the little nodules at the plant roots."},{"startMs":115000,"endMs":120001,"text":"Bacteria in these nodules take nitrogen from the air and change it into a form of plant food."},{"startMs":122001,"endMs":126001,"text":"Other kinds of bacteria are responsible for milk or cream becoming sour."},{"startMs":128001,"endMs":132000,"text":"The development of flavor and other qualities in cheese is a [...]"},{"startMs":132000,"endMs":135000,"text":"[...] disease and the disintegration of dead plants and animals."},{"startMs":136000,"endMs":141001,"text":"In fact, the effects of bacteria are so important that life as we know it would be impossible without them."},{"startMs":142001,"endMs":144001,"text":"Most bacteria are friends of man."},{"startMs":145000,"endMs":149001,"text":"Some even produce chemicals called antibiotics that are used to help fight different diseases."},{"startMs":151000,"endMs":154000,"text":"Only a few kinds of bacteria cause disease."},{"startMs":154001,"endMs":162001,"text":"Probably the first person to observe bacteria was Anton van Leeuwenhoek, inventor of one of the first successful microscopes."},{"startMs":163000,"endMs":168000,"text":"After looking through his crude microscope, he made drawings of the microorganisms he saw."},{"startMs":168001,"endMs":172000,"text":"These represented organisms from tartars scraped from his own teeth."},{"startMs":173000,"endMs":179001,"text":"In contrast, here using a modern microscope, we see other microorganisms taken from tartar."},{"startMs":184000,"endMs":191001,"text":"After the time of Leeuwenhoek, generations were to pass before another achievement of great importance would be made in the study of bacteria."},{"startMs":193001,"endMs":200001,"text":"It was the famed chemist, Louis Pasteur of France, who in the 1850s proved that bacteria caused fermentation."},{"startMs":201001,"endMs":204001,"text":"This explained many of the chemical changes that go on in nature."},{"startMs":205001,"endMs":211001,"text":"Pasteur also proved that bacteria never develop out of dead organic matter alone, as some people had believed."},{"startMs":211001,"endMs":216000,"text":"He emphasized that bacteria can only come from other bacteria."},{"startMs":219001,"endMs":226001,"text":"Soon afterward, it was found that bacteria can spread disease from a sick animal to a healthy animal."},{"startMs":228001,"endMs":233000,"text":"But this discovery was made by Koch, a German contemporary of Pasteur."},{"startMs":233001,"endMs":237000,"text":"This discovery is the basis of the germ theory of disease."},{"startMs":237000,"endMs":242001,"text":"Robert Koch and his assistants also learned to prepare and use nutrient materials."},{"startMs":243000,"endMs":248001,"text":"Nutrient materials are usually jelly-like emulsions or fluids, which favor the growth of bacteria."},{"startMs":249001,"endMs":254000,"text":"Koch made other discoveries that helped lay the foundations of modern bacteriology."},{"startMs":254001,"endMs":259001,"text":"Since the time of Koch, much has been learned and many other kinds of bacteria discovered."},{"startMs":260001,"endMs":268001,"text":"Today, if Ted could collect all known kinds of bacteria, he would have thousands, and the facts that have been discovered about them are exceedingly numerous."},{"startMs":269001,"endMs":273000,"text":"He wonders how a beginner can ever learn about so many kinds of bacteria."},{"startMs":274000,"endMs":276001,"text":"Frank explains that it takes a systematic approach."},{"startMs":277000,"endMs":283000,"text":"For example, bacteria may be separated into groups according to what they live upon, the nature of the food they require."},{"startMs":287001,"endMs":298000,"text":"Some bacteria are parasites, others saprophytes, and still others, autotrophs."},{"startMs":299001,"endMs":303000,"text":"Parasites depend upon living plants or animals for food."},{"startMs":303001,"endMs":305000,"text":"Here are some parasitic bacteria."},{"startMs":306000,"endMs":309001,"text":"Most bacteria that cause human disease are parasitic."},{"startMs":311001,"endMs":315001,"text":"Saphrophites live upon dead plants or animals or their products."},{"startMs":316001,"endMs":320001,"text":"Saphrophitic bacteria produce enzymes that cause the chemical breakdown we call decay."},{"startMs":321001,"endMs":326000,"text":"There are more kinds of saprophytic bacteria than of any other type."},{"startMs":328000,"endMs":332000,"text":"Autotrophs get their energy from minerals or other inorganic materials."},{"startMs":332001,"endMs":336001,"text":"The autotrophic bacteria seen here live upon a form of iron."},{"startMs":336001,"endMs":342001,"text":"Through oxidation, they change one form of iron into another and use the energy that is released."},{"startMs":343001,"endMs":349001,"text":"Unlike the large majority of plants, most bacteria have no chlorophyll with which to manufacture food."},{"startMs":350001,"endMs":355001,"text":"Parasites, saprophytes, and autotrophs all take in food through their cell walls."},{"startMs":356001,"endMs":359001,"text":"To pass through these cell walls, the food must be soluble."},{"startMs":360000,"endMs":367001,"text":"In some instances, insoluble foods are converted into soluble materials by chemicals released by the bacteria themselves."},{"startMs":368000,"endMs":371000,"text":"All bacteria require soluble food to live."},{"startMs":373000,"endMs":380001,"text":"Ted thinks the information seems complicated, but Frank, speaking from experience, explains that the basic facts about bacteria"},{"startMs":380001,"endMs":382000,"text":"are quite simple and very useful."},{"startMs":383000,"endMs":390000,"text":"Besides facts about the food getting of bacteria, Frank describes another type of information that is especially useful in bacteriology."},{"startMs":391000,"endMs":396000,"text":"Depending upon the structure or shape of bacteria, there are four main kinds."},{"startMs":397000,"endMs":401000,"text":"The cocci that are round like these that are common in the air."},{"startMs":402000,"endMs":405001,"text":"The bacilli that are rod shaped like these from gas gangrene."},{"startMs":406001,"endMs":410001,"text":"The spirilla that are spiral like these taken from a sample of well water."},{"startMs":412000,"endMs":415001,"text":"And the filamentous forms such as these thread like ones."},{"startMs":416001,"endMs":424001,"text":"Structure or shape is one of the principal characteristics used by bacteriologists to classify or identify different kinds of bacteria."},{"startMs":427001,"endMs":431000,"text":"The life processes of bacteria are simple."},{"startMs":431001,"endMs":434000,"text":"We've already seen how bacteria obtain food."},{"startMs":434001,"endMs":439001,"text":"Now we will consider their reproduction which takes place through simple division of the cells."},{"startMs":439001,"endMs":444000,"text":"In drawings we can see that bacterium cells are continually growing."},{"startMs":444001,"endMs":447000,"text":"When a cell reaches maturity it divides again."},{"startMs":447001,"endMs":456001,"text":"This goes on and on about every half hour as long as the food supply holds out or until some unfavorable condition slows down or stops their development."},{"startMs":457000,"endMs":461000,"text":"The reproduction rate of bacteria may be greatly affected by temperature."},{"startMs":461001,"endMs":466000,"text":"In a laboratory the temperature can be controlled in an electric incubator."},{"startMs":466000,"endMs":471000,"text":"As Frank explains there's always a temperature that's best for growing bacteria."},{"startMs":471001,"endMs":480000,"text":"Usually this temperature is about 30 to 37 degrees centigrade. 37 degrees centigrade is about equal to the normal human body temperature."},{"startMs":481001,"endMs":489000,"text":"In case the temperature is unfavorable some bacteria especially the rod like kinds have a special means of protecting themselves."},{"startMs":489001,"endMs":491000,"text":"They do it by forming spores."},{"startMs":491001,"endMs":495001,"text":"Notice the spore forming in the center of one of these bacterium cells."},{"startMs":497001,"endMs":502000,"text":"Some other kinds of bacteria form spores at the ends of the rods instead of at the centers."},{"startMs":502001,"endMs":511001,"text":"Either way a spore has a special wall around itself that seems to protect the bacterium inside from such adverse conditions as cold, heat,"},{"startMs":512000,"endMs":513001,"text":"sunlight or disinfectant."},{"startMs":515000,"endMs":520000,"text":"Later when conditions improve the spore may change back again into the rod like bacterium."},{"startMs":520000,"endMs":524000,"text":"Not many disease producing bacteria go through a spore form."},{"startMs":528000,"endMs":534001,"text":"Besides protective mechanisms some bacteria are equipped to move about."},{"startMs":535000,"endMs":539001,"text":"The hair or whip like parts attached to these bacterium cells are called flagella."},{"startMs":540000,"endMs":543000,"text":"Different types of bacteria have different types of flagella."},{"startMs":544000,"endMs":548001,"text":"Such flagella can be moved to propel a living bacterium through blood or other solutions."},{"startMs":548001,"endMs":552000,"text":"Not all bacteria have such means of locomotion."},{"startMs":557000,"endMs":563000,"text":"Since the time of Levenhoek progress in bacteriology has been phenomenal."},{"startMs":563001,"endMs":570001,"text":"His successors such as Pasteur of France and Koch of"},{"startMs":570001,"endMs":577000,"text":"Germany have made discoveries and invented techniques indispensable in successfully preparing nutrient materials."},{"startMs":577001,"endMs":583000,"text":"Growing cultures and subjecting the cultures to tests that help identify and classify bacteria."},{"startMs":585000,"endMs":595000,"text":"Today in bacteriology laboratories work is conducted that leads to the control of the relatively few bacteria that are foes of mankind and to the more effective"},{"startMs":595000,"endMs":602001,"text":"use of the multitudes that are friends in order to make human life more healthful and increase our standard of living."},{"startMs":607000,"endMs":610000,"text":"[...]"}]}